PCB — what the MIT license covers, and what it does not
=======================================================

This repository is MIT-licensed (see LICENSE). That covers the code in this
repository: the local shell (`server/`, `web/`, `shared/`), the tests, the
project site (`site/`) and the documentation.

It does NOT cover the following, because they are not part of this repository:

1. `omp` — the agent engine that does the talking, planning and file editing.
   PCB talks to it as a separate local process (`omp --mode rpc-ui`). `omp` is
   installed by the user and keeps its own license and terms. PCB is a shell
   around it: without `omp` on PATH or installed next to the app, PCB can
   start but cannot answer, plan or change anything.

2. Model providers and model access — the API key and the quota behind it belong
   to the user and to whoever issued them. Keys are configured on the user's
   machine with `omp login`; this repository never ships, stores or forwards a
   key, and the project site never receives one.

3. Editors — desktop VS Code, Cursor, VS Code Insiders and any web build of VS
   Code are licensed by their publishers and are not distributed by this
   repository. The `IDE` button only opens an editor the user already has, and
   the app never downloads one on the user's behalf.

4. The optional Windows shell (WebView2 host) — when it ships it will use
   Microsoft's WebView2 Runtime, which is installed with Windows and carries
   Microsoft's own terms. Repository code that drives it stays MIT.

Third-party packages used by this repository
--------------------------------------------

Build and runtime dependencies, all unmodified, under their own licenses:

| package | license | used by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| react, react-dom | MIT | `web/` |
| vite, @vitejs/plugin-react | MIT | `web/` build |
| typescript | Apache-2.0 | type checking, `src/` build |
| @types/node, @types/react, @types/react-dom | MIT | type checking only |

The project site (`site/`) has no dependencies: it uses only Node's standard
library and serves static files. `site/public/app.js` is written here; nothing
is loaded from a CDN, and no third-party script runs on the page.
